{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790299011,"nonce":"uWU/RCrnQamqnkwaGNoJ2PBrCko=","title":"aggregate_scalarize rescans the whole function for every web it rewrites","message":"run_function calls find_web up to 64 times, and each call rebuilds definitions(func) and runs collect_web (a scan of every block per growth step) for each struct-typed seed until it finds one worth rewriting. The cost is webs x seeds x function size; a function with more than 64 webs keeps the rest as aggregates.\n\nMeasured on the NUC (ZYNTAX_TRACE_OPT_PHASES summed over a run of crates/zyntax_lua/benchmarks/nbody.lua, median of 5, loaded machine): aggregate_scalarize 64 ms on main at 678f49eb, 8.9 ms with the L-number-slots package (which removes most Lua Number tuples before HIR), against 0.9 ms for the same program with Float fields. The remaining nbody webs are 6 of 9 values, 3 of 14 and 3 of 6 in NBody.advance.\n\nFix: find every web in one pass (a use index built once, webs are disjoint), plan and apply each, and re-scan only the members a rewrite exposed (a nested aggregate's field reads).","files":null}]}